The historical context of his fatherhood added dimensions that previous generations had not faced. The late twentieth century was witnessing a revolution in expectations of fatherhood. The model Bob had grown up with\u2014the distant breadwinner, the authoritarian disciplinarian, the man whose primary contribution was financial rather than emotional\u2014was being dismantled by cultural forces that Bob could feel but not fully articulate. He was expected to be present at births, to change diapers, to attend parent-teacher conferences, to know the names of his children's friends and teachers and the specific fears that kept them awake at night. This was not the fathering he had received, and he had no template for it. He was inventing a new kind of fatherhood in real time, learning to be soft in ways his own father had never modeled, learning to say "I love you" when his father had shown love only through provision and protection.

